hey David do you talk a lot about  reading should it be embarrassing not to  read regularly no it shouldn't be  embarrassing because I don't know if  embarrassing or shaming people that  don't read is the best way to get people  to read but I think from an early age it  can become sort of stigmatized to be a  bookworm or to be someone who likes to  read and that's a problem because  reading habits are often determined  pretty early on in life both when it  comes to parents reading to their kids  but also the reaction that kids get in  school when they carry around a book  that you don't have to be reading that  book and you're still reading a book  what that when kids mate get made fun of  for that you're stigmatizing reading in  a way so I don't think it should be  embarrassing not to read but it should  be a completely encouraged activity at  all levels of society a lot of this is  formed during school years and the  problem is that education has become  political in the United States we know  that all else being equal now I'll give  you exceptions in a second but all else  being equal more education leads to  people being more to the left reading  more about the world in an honest way  and I don't mean reading those pop  nonfiction bestsellers like Tucker  Carlson and Ann Coulter and Laura  Ingraham stuff but really reading about  the world reading about behavioral  economics and history and philosophy and  even even reading a diverse selection of  fiction books that is going to have a  liberalizing effect on the world  population and so we've already seen  that the right is sort of planting  planting their feet drawing drawing a  line in the sand on the issue of  Education we had that famous clip from  Rick Santorum from years ago about we  are never going to have the intelligent  people the educated they're just never  going to be on our side and it's not  because of an in doctrine re sort of  circumstance it's because the more you  learn about the world around